SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari’s Address at the 2nd National Tax Dialogue Week","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-03-29","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buharis-address-at-the-2nd-national-tax-dialogue/","claim":"Buhari framed 2022 tax policy around stable domestic revenue mobilization, tax harmonization, clearer FIRS authority, and improved tax-to-GDP performance, while saying the 2021 tax dialogue influenced policy and administrative changes and FIRS surpassed its 2021 collection target.","locator":"paras. 2-13","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_low","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential speech, but self-reported success and goals need outside corroboration for execution claims.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"2022 revenue program and administrative agenda","ruler_attribution":"Direct Buhari policy direction through tax administration; execution attribution is partial because outcomes also depend on the revenue authority and broader macro conditions.","contrary_evidence":["World Bank and IMF later described Nigeria's revenue base as very weak and fiscal space constrained.","The speech is aspirational and does not itself prove sustained implementation."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.6","8B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari’s Speech at the 4th National Summit on Diminishing Corruption in the Public Sector","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-10-04","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buharis-speech-at-the-4th-national-summit-on-diminishing-corruption-in-the-public-sector/","claim":"Buhari said his administration's fight against corruption was a cardinal reform pillar and linked the Open Government Partnership to transparency in public finances and procurement, while urging ICPC action on sexual harassment in education.","locator":"paras. 3-5 and 18-21","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_low","source_confidence_reason":"Direct Buhari speech with clear stated priorities, but it is mainly rhetoric and only limited execution evidence sits in the speech itself.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"2022 reform framing","ruler_attribution":"Direct Buhari statement and reform signal; actual anti-corruption performance needs independent corroboration.","contrary_evidence":["The speech is aspirational and does not by itself show results.","Other evidence shows ongoing corruption and fiscal leakage."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.5","8B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"In Bid to Achieve Universal Coverage, President Buhari Signs National Health Authority Bill Into Law","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-05-19","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/in-bid-to-achieve-universal-coverage-president-buhari-signs-national-health-authority-bill-into-law/","claim":"Buhari signed the National Health Insurance Authority Bill 2022 and said a fund would cover 83 million poor Nigerians, using a vulnerable-group fund, digital records, and state-federal implementation coordination.","locator":"paras. 27-35","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential action with a concrete institutional reform and target population, though delivery still depends on implementation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"direct 2022 policy action","ruler_attribution":"Direct Buhari enactment through presidential assent; implementation is shared with the health ministry, states, and the new authority.","contrary_evidence":["Coverage promises do not prove actual enrollment or service quality in 2022.","The program is partly an institutional design rather than a completed outcome."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.4","8B.6","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Humanitarian Issues: President Buhari Urges More Private Sector Participation, Highlights Intervention Programmes","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-09-23","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/humanitarian-issues-president-buhari-urges-more-private-sector-participation-highlights-intervention-programmes/","claim":"Buhari described the humanitarian ministry as a strategic intervention and listed IDP, flood-response, disability, aging, poverty-reduction, and social-investment frameworks as part of a plan to lift 100 million Nigerians out of poverty in 10 years.","locator":"paras. 27-36","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_low","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential account of policy architecture, but most of the evidence is self-description rather than independent evaluation.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"2022 humanitarian and social-protection framework","ruler_attribution":"Direct Buhari framing of the administration's social-protection and humanitarian program; delivery responsibility is shared with the ministry, agencies, states, and partners.","contrary_evidence":["The ministry was created in 2019, so much of the structure predates 2022.","A stated framework is not proof of effective delivery."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.5","8B.6","8B.9","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Address By President Buhari At The 3rd Ministerial Performance Review Retreat","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-10-17","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/address-by-president-buhari-at-the-3rd-ministerial-performance-review-retreat/","claim":"Buhari said the Central Delivery Coordination Unit was tracking ministerial mandates through weekly updates and quarterly performance reports, and that the administration was on track to reach most deliverables in the 2023 benchmarked targets.","locator":"paras. 30-38 and 79-80","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed official implementation architecture with specific tracking mechanisms, though the assessment of being 'on track' is self-reported.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"direct 2022 implementation review","ruler_attribution":"Direct Buhari oversight of the delivery machinery; the implementation reports reflect administration-wide coordination rather than Buhari alone.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is an internal progress report and may overstate success.","External outcome data later show mixed macro and service-delivery results."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.5","8B.6","8B.8","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Address By President Buhari At The 3rd Ministerial Performance Review Retreat","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-10-17","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/address-by-president-buhari-at-the-3rd-ministerial-performance-review-retreat/","claim":"Buhari reported concrete delivery results including 75% enrollment of Batch C N-Power beneficiaries, school feeding for 9,990,862 pupils, 988,652 BHCPF enrollees, 7,373 accredited primary health facilities, 7,242 financed PHC facilities, and more than 51 million first-dose COVID vaccinations by end-September 2022.","locator":"paras. 60-67","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific implementation counts and institutional outputs are useful, but they are still self-reported administration figures that need outside corroboration.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"direct 2022 implementation evidence","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Buhari's administration, though delivery depended on ministries, agencies, and state partners.","contrary_evidence":["The figures are reported by the administration and should not be treated as independently audited outcomes.","They coexist with severe poverty and service gaps elsewhere in the country."],"lenses":["8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Strong collaboration for better health: Closing immunization gaps in Nigeria through a primary health care approach","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.who.int/about/accountability/results/who-results-report-2022-2023/country-story/2023/strong-collaboration-for-better-health--closing-immunization-gaps-in-nigeria-through-a-primary-health-care-approach","claim":"WHO said Nigeria had over 2.3 million zero-dose children in 2022 and that the government prioritized reaching them through its national immunization and primary health care strategy, while over six million children benefited from immunization services between March 2021 and January 2023.","locator":"paras. 258-273","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent WHO country story with explicit program and implementation details, including state and partner roles.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"covers 2022 and early 2023 implementation of a 2022-relevant program","ruler_attribution":"Only partly direct to Buhari, but it is a Buhari-administration health program executed through NPHCDA and the ministry, so it is relevant to competence.","contrary_evidence":["Nigeria still ranked first among zero-dose countries in 2022, so the problem remained severe.","WHO support and partner financing limited exclusive presidential credit."],"lenses":["8B.6","8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Conduct of Fiscal Policy","publisher":"Central Bank of Nigeria","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://www.cbn.gov.ng/MonetaryPolicy/Fiscal.html","claim":"The CBN says the 2022 budget was based on the 2021-2025 National Development Plan and that in 2022 revenue fell short of budget while capital expenditure came in far below plan, with total expenditure N14.786 trillion versus N18.139 trillion budgeted and capital expenditure N2.203 trillion versus N6.235 trillion budgeted.","locator":"THE 2022 BUDGET and 2022 Budget Performance sections, lines 10-23","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official central-bank summary of 2022 budget design and outturn; it is strong outcome evidence even though macro conditions also mattered.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"direct 2022 budget performance and program basis","ruler_attribution":"The budget framework and broad fiscal direction were under Buhari's administration, but actual performance also depended on oil prices, revenue agencies, and macro shocks.","contrary_evidence":["Low revenue and high debt service limited what could be implemented.","Capital spending shortfall shows partial execution rather than full delivery."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria: 2022 Article IV Consultation-Press Release; Staff Report; and Statement by the Executive Director for Nigeria","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2023-02-16","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2023/02/16/nigeria-2022-article-iv-consultation-press-release-staff-report-and-statement-by-the-529842","claim":"The IMF said Nigeria's economy had reached its pre-crisis level and posted its eighth consecutive quarter of positive growth, but growth was only about 3% in 2022 and inflation remained 21.3%, with floods, oil-sector problems, and higher food prices still weighing on the outlook.","locator":"summary paragraphs 263-266","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral assessment with explicit 2022 period fit; not a measure of Buhari alone but a strong result check against his stated goals.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"assesses 2022 and late-2022 outturns","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Buhari-era macro management; some drivers were inherited or external (oil prices, floods, food and fertilizer shocks).","contrary_evidence":["Positive growth continued despite shocks.","The IMF also notes favorable oil prices and consumption support, so not all progress or failure is attributable to Buhari."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.8","8B.9","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigerian Gross Domestic Product Report (Q4 2022)","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics","publication_date":"2023-02-22","url":"https://www.nigerianstat.gov.ng/elibrary/read/1241288","claim":"Nigeria's GDP grew 3.52% year-on-year in Q4 2022 and 3.10% for the full year, led mainly by services; agriculture grew more slowly because severe floods constrained performance and industry remained negative.","locator":"executive summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary national statistics with clear year-fit; not ruler-specific on its own but strong outcome evidence for the period.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"direct 2022 outcome series, published Feb 2023","ruler_attribution":"The macro outcome reflects Buhari-era policy and shocks together; the floods and oil-sector problems limit direct attribution to the president alone.","contrary_evidence":["GDP remained modest and household welfare did not visibly accelerate.","Growth was driven more by services than by a durable transformation of oil and industry."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"CPI and Inflation Report December 2022","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics","publication_date":"December 2022","url":"https://nigerianstat.gov.ng/elibrary/read/1241274","claim":"Headline inflation was 21.34% in December 2022, up 5.72 percentage points year-on-year from December 2021, showing continued price deterioration by year-end.","locator":"Executive Summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary monthly inflation release with direct year-end outcome; attribution to Buhari is indirect through the macro policy environment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"direct 2022 outcome series","ruler_attribution":"Indirect and shared with monetary authorities and external shocks; it mainly shows the administration failed to prevent price instability.","contrary_evidence":["The rate eased slightly from November 2022.","Inflation was also driven by food and exchange-rate pressures outside direct presidential control."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria’s need to spend more and better","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-11-21","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2022/11/21/nigeria-needs-to-spend-more-and-better","claim":"The World Bank said Nigeria spent only about $220 per person in 2022, social sectors received less than a quarter of the national budget, and regressive fuel, electricity, and exchange-rate subsidies diverted resources away from social services and infrastructure.","locator":"lines 230-250 and 56-57","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent World Bank analysis with direct budget and expenditure comparisons; still country-level rather than Buhari-specific.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 fiscal context and performance","ruler_attribution":"This is largely about Buhari-era fiscal choices and constraints, but some elements were inherited and federal-state shared.","contrary_evidence":["The administration did try to raise revenue and improve service delivery.","The same source notes recent reform efforts on electricity subsidies, so the record was not static."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria's oil output at 32-year low as thieves hobble output","publisher":"MarketScreener / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-09-09","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/quote/stock/SHELL-PLC-130945922/news/Nigeria-s-oil-output-at-32-year-low-as-thieves-hobble-output-41733817/","claim":"Reuters reported that Nigeria's crude output fell below 1 million barrels per day in August 2022 because of rampant pipeline theft and years of underinvestment, threatening public finances.","locator":"lines 1004-1006","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters is strong for outcome reporting; attribution to Buhari is indirect because theft and investment patterns span years and agencies.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"direct 2022 outcome and constraint","ruler_attribution":"Only partly attributable to Buhari, but the administration was responsible for security, investment, and anti-theft response during its tenure.","contrary_evidence":["Low oil output was also driven by long-run underinvestment and criminal theft.","External energy-market conditions inflated the fiscal importance of the sector."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria's NNPC spent $10 billion on fuel subsidy in 2022","publisher":"MarketScreener / Reuters","publication_date":"2023-01-20","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/latest/Nigeria-s-NNPC-spent-10-billion-on-fuel-subsidy-in-2022-42779648/","claim":"Reuters reported that NNPC spent 4.39 trillion naira on petrol subsidy in 2022, did not remit funds to federal accounts, and that the subsidy left a hole in public finances while oil production remained constrained by theft and vandalism.","locator":"lines 846-878","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters-syndicated reporting on a major fiscal drain; the accounting is useful but the causal link to Buhari's personal choice is indirect.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 fiscal outturn reported in early 2023","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Buhari-era fiscal management and subsidy policy, though the spending channel ran through NNPC and prior policy commitments.","contrary_evidence":["The subsidy was politically sensitive and had challenged multiple governments.","Removing it abruptly had political and distributional costs, so some inertia was inherited."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria's public university lecturers suspend strike after eight months","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-10-15","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/10/15/nigeria-strike","claim":"ASUU suspended its eight-month strike after appeals from President Buhari and others, and Reuters noted the government had set aside 470 billion naira for salary enhancements in tertiary institutions.","locator":"Reuters report text in article body","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters-syndicated reporting on a major implementation failure and the government's response; the suspension does not prove the dispute was solved.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"direct 2022 crisis response","ruler_attribution":"Buhari's appeal and budget response are directly attributable, but the strike also reflected entrenched labor disputes and university-funding structure.","contrary_evidence":["The union said issues were not satisfactorily resolved.","The dispute had been ongoing for months, so suspension was not full execution of a reform plan."],"lenses":["8B.3","8B.6","8B.8","8B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"More than 1.5 million children at risk as devastating floods hit Nigeria","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://www.unicef.org/press-releases/more-15-million-children-risk-devastating-floods-hit-nigeria","claim":"UNICEF said the 2022 floods left more than 2.5 million people in Nigeria needing humanitarian assistance, displaced 1.3 million, killed over 600, and damaged over 200,000 homes.","locator":"body text and opening paragraph","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary humanitarian assessment of the crisis size and immediate effects; not ruler-specific, but important for evaluating crisis management.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"direct 2022 shock context","ruler_attribution":"The shock itself was not Buhari's fault, but his administration was responsible for preparedness, mitigation, and relief coordination.","contrary_evidence":["UNICEF also noted ongoing humanitarian response with government and partners.","The magnitude is partly a weather and climate shock, not a governance choice."],"lenses":["8B.9","8B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 30 documents across State House speeches/releases, the CBN fiscal page, NBS GDP and CPI releases, the IMF Article IV package, World Bank fiscal and poverty analysis, Reuters-syndicated reporting on oil output, subsidy costs, and ASUU, plus UNICEF and WHO country reporting.
- I retained 15 new source-claim units from 15 distinct URLs, plus 3 reused items.
- I rejected duplicates and near-duplicates: repeated State House reposts of the same budget and retreat themes, repeated Reuters rewrites of the same oil/subsidy facts, and multiple UNICEF variants covering the same flood response.
- I rejected out-of-period or weakly attributable material when it did not add Buhari-specific execution evidence for 2022.
- I rejected generic macro or governance indicators when they did not establish a Buhari-linked program-to-execution chain.
- I did not treat country-level corruption, poverty, or institutional weakness as competence evidence unless the source tied them to a Buhari policy choice, implementation step, or response.

**Remaining gaps**
- I still do not have direct audit-grade evidence showing Buhari personally overrode weak implementers or replaced them in response to failure; adaptation evidence is mostly indirect.
- The best missing piece is ministry- and procurement-level documentation for 2022 budget execution, especially capital projects and project completion versus formal allocation.
- Attribution remains mixed on macro results like GDP and inflation because oil prices, floods, food shocks, and inherited subsidy structures mattered heavily.
- More search would help on oil-theft countermeasures, the full implementation status of the 2022 health and social-protection laws, and independent evaluation of the Presidential Delivery Tracker / CDCU beyond Buhari's self-report.
